Here is an example of creating a hashtable in Java using the `Hashtable` class which is part of the Java Collections Framework: 

```java
import java.util.Hashtable;

public class Main {
   public static void main(String[] args) {

      // create a hashtable
      Hashtable<String, Integer> hashtable = new Hashtable<>();

      // add values to the hashtable
      hashtable.put("One", 1);
      hashtable.put("Two", 2);
      hashtable.put("Three", 3);
      hashtable.put("Four", 4);
      hashtable.put("Five", 5);

      // print the hashtable contents
      System.out.println("Contents of hash table : " + hashtable);
   }
}
```
This code creates a hashtable object using the `Hashtable` class, with the specified key as a `String` type and value as an `Integer` type. It then adds values to the hashtable using the `put` method. Finally, it prints the contents of the hashtable.
